In economics and econometrics, the Cobb-Douglas prodcution function is a particular function form of the production function, widely used to represent the relationship between the amounts of two or more inputs (particularly physical labor and capital) and the amount of output that can be produced by those inputs. 
The Cobb-Douglas form was developed and tested against statistical evidence by Charles Cob and Paul Dougalas between 1927 and 1947; according to Douglas, the functional form itself was developed earlier by Philip Wicksteed. 
